        "files_changed": [
          "agent/core/src/main/java/org/jolokia/util/IoUtil.java",
          "agent/core/src/main/java/org/jolokia/http/AgentServlet.java",
          "agent/jvm/src/main/java/org/jolokia/jvmagent/handler/JolokiaHttpHandler.java",
          "agent/core/src/test/java/org/jolokia/util/IoUtilTest.java",
          "agent/core/src/test/java/org/jolokia/http/AgentServletTest.java",
          "agent/jvm/src/test/java/org/jolokia/jvmagent/handler/JolokiaHttpHandlerTest.java"
        ],
        "patch_notes": "Root cause: the `callback` query/POST parameter is fully attacker-controlled and was concatenated verbatim into the response body that is served with a `text/javascript` content type (`callback + \"(\" + json + \");\"` in AgentServlet.sendAllJSON and JolokiaHttpHandler.sendAllJSON, and `pWriter.write(callback)` in IoUtil.streamResponseAndClose used by both agents' streaming paths). An attacker could set `callback=</script><script>alert(document.cookie)</script>` (or similar) to get arbitrary script reflected and executed in the victim's browser (reflected XSS). Fix: added `IoUtil.isValidCallback(String)`, a single shared validator using the regex `^[a-zA-Z_$][a-zA-Z0-9_$]*(\\.[a-zA-Z_$][a-zA-Z0-9_$]*)*$` (a plain, optionally dot-namespaced JS identifier — the universal safe shape for JSONP callback names used by jQuery, AngularJS, ExtJS, etc.). This is applied: (1) inside `IoUtil.streamResponseAndClose`, covering both AgentServlet's and JolokiaHttpHandler's streaming sinks; (2) in `AgentServlet.sendAllJSON`; (3) in `JolokiaHttpHandler.sendAllJSON`. When the callback fails validation, the code no longer wraps/echoes it — it falls back to emitting plain JSON (no function-call wrapper), which is inert if reflected. Content-type selection (`text/javascript` vs configured mime type) was intentionally left unchanged since it isn't the injection vector and altering it risked breaking existing JSONP-consuming clients; a plain JSON body served as `text/javascript` when a callback param happens to be present but invalid is harmless. Added regression tests: IoUtilTest (new `checkWriteWithMaliciousCallbackIsNotInjected` and `checkIsValidCallback`), AgentServletTest (new `withMaliciousCallback`, mirroring the existing `withCallback` test), and JolokiaHttpHandlerTest (new `testMaliciousCallbackGet`, mirroring the existing `testCallbackGet`). All new tests pass, and the existing valid-callback tests (`withCallback`, `testCallbackGet`, `testCallbackPost`, `checkWriteWithCallback`) still pass unmodified, confirming no regression for legitimate JSONP usage.",
        "regression_commands": [
          "mvn -B -pl agent/core -am test -Dtest=IoUtilTest,AgentServletTest -DfailIfNoTests=false",
          "mvn -B -pl agent/jvm -am test -Dtest=JolokiaHttpHandlerTest -DfailIfNoTests=false"
        ],
        "risk_notes": "Low risk, narrowly scoped change confined to the JSONP callback-handling code path shared by the servlet and JVM agents. Behavior for any callback name matching the standard JS-identifier shape (the overwhelming majority of real-world JSONP callback names) is completely unchanged. The only behavior change is for callback values that were never valid JavaScript identifiers to begin with (i.e., values that could only have been used maliciously or by a broken client) — these now silently degrade to a plain JSON response instead of being reflected as executable script. Note: the pre-existing `agent/jvm` test module (`JolokiaHttpHandlerTest`) has several tests (including ones unrelated to this change, e.g. `invalidMethod`, `customRestrictor`) failing in this container with `java.lang.NullPointerException` at `com.sun.net.httpserver.Headers.put` inside the test helper `prepareExchange` — this was verified to also fail identically on an unmodified clone of the baseline commit, confirming it is a pre-existing JDK/test-environment issue in this Docker image, not something introduced or worsened by this patch.",
        "status": "patched",
        "summary": "Fixed reflected XSS (CWE-79) via the JSONP `callback` request parameter, which was written unescaped into the JavaScript response body in both the servlet agent (AgentServlet) and the JVM agent (JolokiaHttpHandler), for both the streaming (IoUtil.streamResponseAndClose) and non-streaming (sendAllJSON) response paths. Added a shared IoUtil.isValidCallback() validator that only accepts safe, plain (optionally dotted) JavaScript identifiers as a JSONP callback name, and applied it at all four sink locations (the two AgentServlet.java sinks and the two JolokiaHttpHandler.java sinks) plus inside IoUtil.streamResponseAndClose itself as defense-in-depth. Any callback value that doesn't match the safe identifier pattern (e.g. containing `<`, `>`, `(`, `)`, quotes, whitespace, newlines, etc.) is no longer written into the response verbatim; instead the response silently falls back to plain (non-JSONP) JSON output, preserving normal behavior for legitimate callback names like `myCallback`, `jQuery123_456`, or `angular.callbacks._0`."
      }
    }
